blican lean and the open seat left by term-limited Laura Kelly favor Masterson; polls since mid-September put him up four to eight points, and raters and markets alike make Holscher a clear underdog. Holscher's path runs through Johnson County moderates, but no public evidence yet shows her closing the gap.", "watch": "A sustained Democratic ad campaign highlighting Masterson's debate absences could consolidate Kansas City suburban moderates and tighten the race in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Laura Kelly (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kansas is choosing a successor to term-limited Governor Laura Kelly, and the open seat has shifted the contest toward Republicans. Senate President Ty Masterson leads Democrat Cindy Holscher in every public poll since mid-September, by margins of four to eight points, with independent voters breaking his way in recent surveys. Holscher, a Johnson County moderate, is trying to rebuild Kelly's winning coalition in the Kansas City suburbs.\n\nSeven national raters call the race Lean Republican or better for the GOP, and prediction markets give Holscher roughly a one-in-four chance. National Democrats have begun advertising against Masterson, who has skipped two televised debates, giving Holscher an argument that he is avoiding scrutiny. Still, the state's eight-point Republican lean and Kelly's absence from the ballot leave Holscher needing a late shift among moderates to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1817, "margin": -4.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.161, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1643, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1648, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1739, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.215, "margin": -4.3}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -4.0}]}